WEBVTT

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:16.030
The second match of 
Washizu Mahjong has begun.

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:20.210
However, Akagi rejects a rare opportunity

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:22.960
to regain some of the blood 
he lost during the game.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:26.050
He has chosen to focus solely on

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:29.790
taking the last of Washizu's 
money, destroying him.

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:34.470
I don't need it.

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:42.230
Why, Akagi?!

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:43.560
Why?

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:46.680
No matter how you slice it, 
you've got to get that blood back!

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:48.270
Akagi!

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:49.550
Money.

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:52.290
I'm going to only grab 
money here like a miser.

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:57.290
Only then can I chase down Washizu.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:01.220
Only then can I...

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:03.710
kill Washizu.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:09.220
<b>Episode 20</b>

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:09.220
<b>Hope and Folly</b>

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:11.940
In the end, Akagi enters the second 
match without returning those

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:13.650
1,100 CCs of blood into his body.

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:19.310
<b>- South Hand 2 -</b>

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:20.900
<i>It's inconceivable.</i>

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:24.170
<i>Why won't you replenish your blood?</i>

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:26.320
<i>Why don't you return the blood you lost?</i>

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:30.820
<i>Why don't you try to get 
out of this danger zone?</i>

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:34.410
<i>Why do you keep yourself in this position,</i>

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:37.970
<i>where you could die any time 
you deal into a mangan hand?</i>

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:38.830
<i>Akagi!</i>

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:43.250
<i>I don't understand.</i>

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:46.040
<i>This is something that I can't understand.</i>

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:50.570
<i>But if I had to force myself 
to imagine a reason...</i>

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:54.400
<i>I'd guess that by staying 
in this danger zone,</i>

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:57.350
<i>Akagi has something to gain.</i>

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:04.090
<i>It's as if the danger itself is 
honing something deep within him.</i>

00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:08.460
<i>Is it a matter of that sort of 
sense, that sort of sensitivity?</i>

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:13.120
<i>Or is it meant to rattle Washizu?</i>

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:17.100
<i>By continuing this reckless act...</i>

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:23.240
<i>he might be trying to instill 
an indescribable fear into Washizu.</i>

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:26.910
<i>Is he trying to make him nervous, 
to put him under pressure?</i>

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:30.600
<i>I don't know.</i>

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:33.430
<i>There's no way an ordinary guy 
like me can understand</i>

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:35.350
<i>what someone like Akagi is thinking.</i>

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:41.330
<i>Perhaps my speculations are 
completely off the mark.</i>

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:45.090
<i>But no matter what his reasons are,</i>

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:48.370
<i>his refusal of the transfusion is very risky.</i>

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:54.310
<i>It's the kind of act that could 
result in death from one misstep.</i>

00:03:54.310 --> 00:03:57.330
<i>To speak plainly, it's close to suicide.</i>

00:03:57.330 --> 00:04:01.730
<i>What kind of a reckless, foolish, 
idiotic strategy is this?</i>

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:06.000
<i>It's all over the moment 
he deals into a mangan hand.</i>

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:12.380
Pinfu, tsumo, ittsuu, one dora. Mangan.

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:19.030
But despite Ougi's concern, 
the flow of the game favors Akagi.

00:04:19.030 --> 00:04:21.530
With this win, he overturns Washizu's lead.

00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:23.750
He leads by 12,000 points.

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:25.770
This time, he takes 4,000 
points from Washizu.

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:28.680
Of course, he does not ask 
for any of his blood back.

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:30.270
On to South Hand 3.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:36.280
In South Hand 3, he sees that 
Washizu is close to tenpai.

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:40.780
Ron!

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:49.290
Akagi plays into Yasuoka's hand 
and kills Washizu's chance.

00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:53.790
He ends the hand quickly 
to avoid danger.

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:57.960
The last hand, South Hand 4.

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:03.040
<i>He keeps making it through.</i>

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:06.540
<i>At one point, he was over 
20,000 points behind.</i>

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:10.410
<i>When he finally caught up, he didn't 
take any of his blood back.</i>

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:12.290
<i>That made me pretty nervous,</i>

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:15.810
<i>but I've come to realize 
that luck's on his side.</i>

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:18.940
<i>Only one hand left.</i>

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:23.490
If he wins this hand, he will survive.

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:25.220
The second match will end.

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:29.000
Ougi puts his hopes on this last hand.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:32.730
This is Akagi's starting hand.

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:36.110
<i>So-so.</i>

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:39.740
<i>It's not the best hand, but if he gets 
some good draws, he could end it quickly.</i>

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:42.910
<i>The problem is Washizu.</i>

00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:45.980
<i>He's 9,200 points behind right now.</i>

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:49.150
<i>The most frightening prospect 
is him getting a mangan tsumo.</i>

00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:52.670
<i>So long as that doesn't happen, we win.</i>

00:05:57.310 --> 00:05:58.250
<i>Nice!</i>

00:05:58.250 --> 00:05:59.160
<i>It looks bad for him.</i>

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:02.730
<i>We're almost there.</i>

00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:03.750
<i>We can do this.</i>

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:05.160
<i>Akagi will most likely win!</i>

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:12.550
Seeing Washizu's starting 
tiles makes Ougi relax.

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:15.670
But Akagi is not so relaxed.

00:06:15.670 --> 00:06:20.740
Unlike Ougi, he felt the 
exact opposite of Ougi.

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:25.790
A premonition.

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:32.160
This tile felt foreign in his hand...

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:36.160
<i>There's a lump I have 
to cut out to win here.</i>

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:40.900
It's the last hand of the second 
match in Washizu Mahjong.

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:48.410
On the first discard, 
Akagi uncharacteristically pauses.

00:06:48.910 --> 00:06:50.530
<i>What's going on?</i>

00:06:50.530 --> 00:06:53.910
<i>There's nothing particularly 
worrisome here.</i>

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:56.770
<i>You're 9,200 points ahead of Washizu.</i>

00:06:56.770 --> 00:07:01.420
<i>So long as Washizu doesn't get 
a mangan tsumo, it's all good.</i>

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:05.420
<i>Then we can end the 
second match in the lead.</i>

00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:07.660
<i>His victory would be certain.</i>

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:13.070
<i>And we're lucky; Washizu's 
hand doesn't look so great.</i>

00:07:14.230 --> 00:07:18.670
<i>He'll be lucky if he can win at all, 
let alone get that hand into a mangan.</i>

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:22.430
<i>I get it. It's the dora!</i>

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:23.910
<i>The dora is the west wind.</i>

00:07:24.670 --> 00:07:27.620
<i>And since Washizu is the west player, 
it's worth more for him now!</i>

00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:30.600
<i>If he calls that, he's 
already guaranteed a mangan.</i>

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:35.560
<i>He's got a ticket for a mangan. 
He's got a trump card for this game,</i>

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:39.660
<i>and he's got one in his starting hand.</i>

00:07:40.910 --> 00:07:43.890
<i>No, it might be more than one.</i>

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:48.400
<i>If that black tile is also a west wind, 
he'll already have a pair.</i>

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:55.140
<i>Which would make this west wind 
in Akagi's hand a ticking bomb.</i>

00:07:56.580 --> 00:07:58.540
<i>A single demon lurking in his own ranks...</i>

00:07:58.540 --> 00:08:00.280
<i>Damn it, what are you going to do?</i>

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:06.400
<i>One false move with this west wind 
and it will end up costing his life.</i>

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:09.760
<i>Does he have no choice but to keep it?</i>

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:12.070
<i>So long as Washizu doesn't give it up,</i>

00:08:12.070 --> 00:08:14.940
<i>you could hold on to it 
until the end of the game.</i>

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:18.300
<i>If you're lucky, 
you might even get a pair.</i>

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:23.340
<i>If that doesn't happen, you would 
be forced into a single west wind wait.</i>

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:26.090
<i>You have to be prepared for the possibility.</i>

00:08:26.090 --> 00:08:30.330
<i>But holding on to it might 
just be the safest bet.</i>

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:33.510
<i>If not, though, will you discard it now?</i>

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:36.640
<i>He's most likely thinking 
about the same thing.</i>

00:08:37.050 --> 00:08:40.150
<i>It'll be fine as long as Washizu 
doesn't have two of them.</i>

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:44.990
<i>He has the one, but it's not 
likely that he has another one.</i>

00:08:44.990 --> 00:08:49.170
<i>Then it would be best to discard 
it before Washizu gets a pair.</i>

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:53.310
<i>At least then he'd get away 
from the curse of this west wind.</i>

00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:57.300
<i>But should the opposite be true...</i>

00:08:57.300 --> 00:09:00.040
<i>should Washizu already 
have two west winds...</i>

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:02.670
<i>That would be the absolute worst move.</i>

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:08.170
<i>A folly that would allow Washizu 
to call his own wind, and the dora.</i>

00:09:08.170 --> 00:09:12.890
<i>It's like giving your enemy a 
knife and saying "please kill me."</i>

00:09:12.890 --> 00:09:15.490
<i>It would be an irreversible error.</i>

00:09:16.590 --> 00:09:17.680
<i>What are you going to do?</i>

00:09:18.110 --> 00:09:19.570
<i>What should you do?</i>

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:21.860
<i>What should you...</i>

00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:26.190
<i>Akagi!</i>

00:09:32.090 --> 00:09:34.870
Akagi's first discard is a 9 of bamboo.

00:09:34.870 --> 00:09:36.170
He did not discard the west wind.

00:09:38.090 --> 00:09:40.540
<i>He's going to wait and see? 
Oh, that makes sense.</i>

00:09:40.540 --> 00:09:43.250
<i>It's not too late to discard the west wind</i>

00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:45.090
<i>after he's observed Washizu for a while.</i>

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:52.650
No sooner than Ougi thought this, the situation abruptly changes.

00:09:53.080 --> 00:09:54.150
Chi!

00:09:58.060 --> 00:09:59.190
And then...

00:10:01.690 --> 00:10:02.590
Pon!

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:13.040
<i>This can't be...</i>

00:10:14.290 --> 00:10:17.270
Within five seconds of Akagi 
discarding that first tile,

00:10:17.670 --> 00:10:19.970
Washizu calls twice.

00:10:19.970 --> 00:10:22.010
His hand progresses rapidly.

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:26.020
What's more, it's a potential green dragon 
honitsu with two dora, a five han hand.

00:10:27.390 --> 00:10:30.220
It's entirely possible to take 
the lead with a mangan tsumo.

00:10:30.220 --> 00:10:32.190
And that hand seems to be almost ready.

00:10:33.690 --> 00:10:35.420
<i>No! It can't be!</i>

00:10:35.420 --> 00:10:36.760
<i>How is this possible?</i>

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:40.760
<i>I don't think he's in tenpai yet, but...</i>

00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:44.500
<i>there's no way Akagi can 
discard the west wind now.</i>

00:10:44.500 --> 00:10:49.390
<i>If he does and Washizu calls it, he could 
get a green dragon honitsu with three dora.</i>

00:10:49.390 --> 00:10:50.010
<i>A haneman.</i>

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:54.030
<i>He's 9,200 points behind right now,</i>

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:56.190
<i>so he wouldn't even need 
a tsumo with that haneman.</i>

00:10:56.190 --> 00:10:59.520
<i>Having Suzuki deal into his hand 
would be enough to take the lead.</i>

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:01.080
<i>He'll crumble...</i>

00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:03.660
<i>Akagi will die!</i>

00:11:14.970 --> 00:11:16.530
<i>D-Don't tell me...</i>

00:11:25.150 --> 00:11:26.310
<i>Don't tell me...</i>

00:11:30.910 --> 00:11:32.590
<i>I can't believe this.</i>

00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:35.780
<i>From the starting hands, I was 
sure we'd make an easy getaway.</i>

00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:41.020
<i>It only took a few seconds for 
Washizu to back him into a corner.</i>

00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:46.030
<i>It's like he looked up and 
death is right behind him.</i>

00:11:46.970 --> 00:11:48.530
<i>Akagi!</i>

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:57.770
At this point, even Ougi realizes 
that the abnormal is normal here.

00:11:58.140 --> 00:12:00.570
The special rule of Washizu Mahjong,

00:12:00.570 --> 00:12:05.310
betting one's life on a Mahjong 
game, is already abnormal.

00:12:07.950 --> 00:12:10.580
Which means that in this abnormal world,

00:12:10.580 --> 00:12:14.590
it only makes sense to regard 
the abnormal as normal.

00:12:15.370 --> 00:12:19.690
<i>Akagi has understood this from the start.</i>

00:12:19.690 --> 00:12:23.560
<i>He knew that his life is at risk 
in this game of Washizu Mahjong.</i>

00:12:24.230 --> 00:12:28.930
<i>You'll be pushed to the very brink 
and are forced to continue playing.</i>

00:12:28.930 --> 00:12:34.570
<i>Being able to maintain your 
concentration is your lifeline.</i>

00:12:34.570 --> 00:12:40.390
<i>He also knew that thinking 
you were safe from a mangan hand</i>

00:12:40.390 --> 00:12:44.350
<i>just because you had your blood 
transfused back into your body</i>

00:12:44.350 --> 00:12:47.300
<i>was the wrong way of thinking.</i>

00:12:47.300 --> 00:12:50.590
<i>He made his choice 
based on the situation.</i>

00:12:50.590 --> 00:12:54.170
<i>He knows that he must change his 
game according to the situation.</i>

00:12:54.170 --> 00:12:56.570
<i>Thinking "I'll be all right 
even if he gets a mangan,</i>

00:12:56.570 --> 00:12:58.380
<i>so I can discard this risky tile,"</i>

00:12:58.380 --> 00:12:59.560
<i>is the normal way to think.</i>

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:06.810
<i>It's fine for a normal game, 
but it's too weak for Washizu Mahjong.</i>

00:13:07.830 --> 00:13:10.070
That's a thought that 
one must not give in to.

00:13:10.540 --> 00:13:11.810
It ultimately means that one

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:15.280
doesn't really mind dealing 
into their opponent's hand.

00:13:16.330 --> 00:13:20.040
As a result, you stop trying to 
read your opponents completely

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:22.860
and you will play the 
odds on certain tiles.

00:13:22.860 --> 00:13:25.820
You will start making half-hearted 
guesses about the opponent's hand.

00:13:26.780 --> 00:13:28.880
Both foresight and 
determination deteriorate.

00:13:29.630 --> 00:13:33.020
Playing Mahjong like that leads to 
failure at the most crucial moment.

00:13:34.330 --> 00:13:38.040
Impurities will start to filter 
into the thought process.

00:13:38.570 --> 00:13:39.570
That is to say...

00:13:42.300 --> 00:13:43.570
...fear.

00:13:45.380 --> 00:13:47.810
<i>He can't let that creep into him.</i>

00:13:48.380 --> 00:13:51.940
<i>It will make its way in if he 
relaxes even for an instant.</i>

00:13:51.940 --> 00:13:55.520
<i>He'll be devoured by fear.</i>

00:13:56.040 --> 00:14:00.800
<i>I thought I had grasped that properly,</i>

00:14:00.800 --> 00:14:04.270
<i>but compared to Akagi, 
my comprehension lacked severely.</i>

00:14:04.990 --> 00:14:08.410
<i>Had I understood it, 
I never would've been so naïve</i>

00:14:08.410 --> 00:14:11.470
<i>as to consider the blood transfusion.</i>

00:14:11.470 --> 00:14:15.180
<i>The problem is, this hunger for 
safety itself is like a cancer</i>

00:14:15.180 --> 00:14:19.210
<i>that will eventually consume your mind.</i>

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:23.680
<i>Pursuing safety and 
finding a temporary haven</i>

00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:25.330
<i>will relax your mind for a while.</i>

00:14:25.330 --> 00:14:27.830
<i>That's the way the mind works.</i>

00:14:27.830 --> 00:14:28.720
<i>It cannot be helped.</i>

00:14:29.110 --> 00:14:32.970
<i>But it's exactly that feeling 
of relief that is bad.</i>

00:14:32.970 --> 00:14:37.620
<i>In fact, since I was so 
certain of Akagi's victory,</i>

00:14:37.620 --> 00:14:41.460
<i>this predicament has me 
all the more shaken.</i>

00:14:42.860 --> 00:14:47.240
<i>An agitated mind cannot possibly 
make cold calculations.</i>

00:14:47.650 --> 00:14:49.970
<i>There is no way to keep calm 
and take countermeasures.</i>

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:54.980
<i>You can't let your guard down, 
not even for an instant.</i>

00:14:54.980 --> 00:14:59.480
<i>Akagi understands that far 
better than I ever could.</i>

00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:01.410
<i>This is our salvation.</i>

00:15:01.410 --> 00:15:03.720
<i>His unwavering composure is our salvation.</i>

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:08.220
<i>His utter tranquility despite 
this situation is our salvation.</i>

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:13.230
<i>He's most likely not making any 
shallow reads of Washizu's hand.</i>

00:15:13.920 --> 00:15:17.970
<i>He's playing with a black or white mentality.</i>

00:15:17.970 --> 00:15:20.120
<i>There are no ambiguities.</i>

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:24.270
<i>Wait a second.</i>

00:15:24.970 --> 00:15:33.070
<i>Since Akagi didn't discard that west wind, and kept it in his hand...</i>

00:15:33.070 --> 00:15:43.190
<i>That means he assumes Washizu 
has not just one, but two west winds.</i>

00:15:43.590 --> 00:15:45.010
<i>There's no doubt about it.</i>

00:15:45.010 --> 00:15:47.790
<i>This is the genius Akagi's intuition.</i>

00:15:47.790 --> 00:15:48.930
<i>His sense...</i>

00:15:50.370 --> 00:15:51.960
<i>Damn it.</i>

00:15:51.960 --> 00:15:54.930
<i>If he's right, we've got 
the worst-case scenario.</i>

00:15:55.700 --> 00:15:59.700
<i>On top of those two calls, 
he's got two west winds.</i>

00:15:59.700 --> 00:16:03.560
<i>He no longer needs to get 
a tsumo to take the lead.</i>

00:16:03.560 --> 00:16:05.980
<i>He's all clear for a haneman.</i>

00:16:05.980 --> 00:16:07.180
<i>Which means...</i>

00:16:09.100 --> 00:16:10.460
<i>annihilation!</i>

00:16:10.460 --> 00:16:11.920
<i>Death is nigh!</i>

00:16:13.260 --> 00:16:18.250
Unfortunately, Ougi's thoughts and 
Akagi's intuition were right on the mark.

00:16:21.190 --> 00:16:22.720
They are reality.

00:16:23.530 --> 00:16:27.670
And with this latest draw, 
Washizu's hand progresses yet again.

00:16:31.250 --> 00:16:33.170
<i>I've got you now.</i>

00:16:33.680 --> 00:16:36.670
<i>One more step and I'll kill you.</i>

00:16:37.570 --> 00:16:40.180
<i>You cocky little brat.</i>

00:16:41.070 --> 00:16:44.680
<i>Tremble before me, Akagi.</i>

00:16:46.060 --> 00:16:47.840
The situation is bleak indeed.

00:16:48.270 --> 00:16:51.250
Washizu no longer needs a tsumo 
nor a direct hit to take the lead.

00:16:51.250 --> 00:16:54.480
He can cause an upset even if his 
underling deals into his hand.

00:16:56.130 --> 00:16:58.660
But even if Akagi wanted 
to attempt anything,

00:16:58.660 --> 00:17:01.490
his hand is far from tenpai.

00:17:02.780 --> 00:17:03.700
No.

00:17:03.700 --> 00:17:08.320
Even if he were to get great 
draws and get into tenpai,

00:17:08.320 --> 00:17:10.170
he cannot discard this west wind.

00:17:11.480 --> 00:17:14.870
<i>So essentially, he can't get 
into tenpai unless he goes</i>

00:17:14.870 --> 00:17:19.080
<i>for a single wait for the west 
 wind or can complete the pair.</i>

00:17:19.680 --> 00:17:21.200
Among all this...

00:17:27.530 --> 00:17:28.920
<i>Pon!</i>

00:17:29.950 --> 00:17:33.340
Washizu moves forward by 
calling this 1 of circles.

00:17:33.340 --> 00:17:34.930
He's called three times 
without slowing down.

00:17:36.680 --> 00:17:40.170
And then, the circle tiles start to flow.

00:17:40.730 --> 00:17:44.030
A honitsu tenpai becomes 
more and more likely.

00:17:44.990 --> 00:17:46.530
By now the possibility of the haneman

00:17:46.530 --> 00:17:48.180
that will cause the 
upset is extremely real.

00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:53.400
<i>It's over.</i>

00:17:53.400 --> 00:17:55.680
<i>There's nothing he can do.</i>

00:17:56.230 --> 00:17:59.770
<i>It might even end before his next draw.</i>

00:18:01.190 --> 00:18:04.690
<i>If Suzuki draws the west wind...</i>

00:18:13.310 --> 00:18:14.890
<i>We've survived.</i>

00:18:14.890 --> 00:18:17.450
<i>But it's still hopeless.</i>

00:18:17.450 --> 00:18:18.940
<i>The gap is too wide.</i>

00:18:19.320 --> 00:18:21.850
<i>If Akagi was at least in tenpai, 
he might be able to</i>

00:18:21.850 --> 00:18:24.750
<i>sneak through without 
discarding the west wind.</i>

00:18:26.450 --> 00:18:29.460
<i>But he's still two tiles from tenpai.</i>

00:18:34.840 --> 00:18:36.460
<i>And he keeps drawing useless tiles.</i>

00:18:36.930 --> 00:18:40.060
<i>He's still working on getting 
rid of his honor tiles.</i>

00:18:40.060 --> 00:18:42.850
<i>Maybe we have to rely 
on Yasuoka here.</i>

00:18:44.300 --> 00:18:47.470
<i>If he wins quickly, that could work, too.</i>

00:18:48.430 --> 00:18:51.240
<i>So long as Akagi has that 
west wind he can't discard,</i>

00:18:51.480 --> 00:18:53.710
<i>Yasuoka is our only hope.</i>

00:18:56.030 --> 00:19:00.330
But Yasuoka is also not in tenpai. 
He's two tiles away.

00:19:03.260 --> 00:19:05.460
<i>Is there nothing we can do?</i>

00:19:05.460 --> 00:19:06.950
<i>Is praying all we can do?</i>

00:19:07.610 --> 00:19:10.690
<i>Pray that our enemy does 
not draw the deciding tile?</i>

00:19:12.900 --> 00:19:14.930
<i>Aimlessly pray...</i>

00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:22.630
<i>Are we forced to sit idly by... 
while praying that...</i>

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:25.640
<i>he'll keep drawing useless tiles?</i>

00:19:25.640 --> 00:19:29.220
<i>And that while he keeps 
drawing useless tiles,</i>

00:19:29.220 --> 00:19:34.710
<i>either Akagi or Yasuoka will 
somehow get into tenpai?</i>

00:19:35.070 --> 00:19:41.750
<i>Or that Washizu can't get his winning 
tile and have the game end in a draw...</i>

00:19:41.750 --> 00:19:45.630
<i>That we see the end of 
this hand without Washizu</i>

00:19:45.630 --> 00:19:47.490
<i>getting a tsumo or Suzuki 
dealing into his hand.</i>

00:19:48.400 --> 00:19:50.230
<i>That's all we can hope for!</i>

00:19:51.100 --> 00:19:51.810
<i>No.</i>

00:19:51.810 --> 00:19:56.230
<i>That's relying too much on the 
others and it is way too unrealistic.</i>

00:19:57.120 --> 00:20:02.240
<i>When we consider that Washizu's 
hand just needs someone to deal into it,</i>

00:20:03.060 --> 00:20:06.240
<i>we don't have the time 
to say things like that.</i>

00:20:06.770 --> 00:20:08.200
<i>We need to get into tenpai.</i>

00:20:10.270 --> 00:20:11.750
<i>It's the only way.</i>

00:20:12.200 --> 00:20:15.670
<i>To get into tenpai before the 
enemy draws the decisive tile.</i>

00:20:17.010 --> 00:20:20.490
<i>We have get into tenpai somehow.</i>

00:20:23.490 --> 00:20:27.010
And yet, the one tile Ougi 
wanted to be rid of,

00:20:27.830 --> 00:20:31.000
the tile he wanted his own 
party to draw and kill off,

00:20:31.790 --> 00:20:35.740
the tile that will decide the 
fate of Akagi and Washizu...

00:20:36.740 --> 00:20:38.320
is drawn by Suzuki.

00:20:47.930 --> 00:20:50.280
<i>This can't be happening.</i>

00:20:50.280 --> 00:20:53.490
<i>The enemy... the enemy 
got the west wind!</i>

00:20:55.270 --> 00:20:59.450
Right then, they all foresaw 
Akagi's impending death.

00:21:01.150 --> 00:21:04.560
They all foresaw it, 
except for Akagi himself.

00:22:16.310 --> 00:22:18.900
Next time, "A Ray of Illusion."

00:22:17.710 --> 00:22:20.550
<b>Next Episode</b>

00:22:17.710 --> 00:22:20.550
<b>A Ray of Illusion</b>
